# Response headers for www.webhook.co. Next copies public/* into the export, so this ships as
# out/_headers, which Cloudflare Workers Static Assets honors natively. The worker only ever serves
# www.webhook.co (workers_dev:false; the apex is a separate zone redirect), so these host-wide rules
# never reach api./mcp. — which is why HSTS can live here without includeSubDomains.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  # Two years, www-only. NO includeSubDomains / preload: this zone also fronts api.webhook.co and
  # mcp.webhook.co, and HSTS with includeSubDomains is sticky and can't be cleanly walked back.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000
  # 'unsafe-inline' on script-src is required: Next's static export hydrates via inline
  # self.__next_f.push(...) scripts, and a static _headers file can't mint per-request nonces — a
  # bare 'self' would still return 200 but silently break interactivity. style-src inline covers
  # Next/Geist's injected styles. Tighten to per-build hashes later if we add a build step for it.
  #
  # The two cloudflareinsights entries carry the Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon (see
  # src/components/marketing/web-analytics.tsx). They are a MATCHED PAIR and both are required:
  # script-src loads beacon.min.js, and connect-src is where it uploads. This host is a Workers custom
  # domain whose /cdn-cgi/rum 404s, so the beacon is embedded manually and uploads CROSS-ORIGIN to
  # cloudflareinsights.com — 'self' does not cover it. Drop either and the beacon dies silently.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com; frame-src https://status.webhook.co; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'

# /play needs to reach the isolated sandbox worker (play.wbhk.my) for the mint fetch + the SSE
# stream (connect-src), and to load the Turnstile challenge (script/frame/connect to
# challenges.cloudflare.com). This override is SCOPED to /play so the rest of the site keeps the tight
# global policy. Exact hosts only, no wildcards.
#
# ⚠️ THE `!` LINE IS LOAD-BEARING. Cloudflare _headers rules are ADDITIVE: without it, /play is served
# with BOTH this policy and the /* one. Two CSP headers are not "most specific wins" — a browser
# enforces their INTERSECTION, so the global `script-src 'self'` still blocked
# challenges.cloudflare.com and the Turnstile script never loaded, leaving the mint button disabled
# forever ("failed to load the Turnstile script"). Unset the inherited header, then set ours.
# The /* security headers (nosniff, HSTS, Referrer-Policy, …) are separate headers and still apply.
#
# ⚠️ AND BECAUSE IT UNSETS, THIS BLOCK MUST RE-STATE EVERY GLOBAL ALLOWANCE IT STILL NEEDS. /play is a
# page like any other, so it renders the same <WebAnalytics /> beacon from the root layout — the
# cloudflareinsights entries below are NOT duplication, they are the global policy's entries that the
# `!` above just threw away. Omit them and analytics is dead on exactly one page, which is precisely
# the kind of gap nothing else would catch.
/play
  ! Content-Security-Policy
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com https://play.wbhk.my https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://status.webhook.co; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'

# Only /_next/static/* is content-hashed (JS/CSS chunks + the self-hosted Geist fonts under
# /_next/static/media/*), so only it is safe to cache immutably. Cloudflare otherwise defaults
# static assets to max-age=0; HTML keeps that default so deploys go live immediately.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The answer-engine index (llmstxt.org). Its prose is UTF-8 — em dashes, curly quotes — and `text/plain`
# is the ONE content type on this site that cannot say so in-band: HTML declares its encoding with
# `<meta charset>` and XML with `<?xml encoding?>`, both of which a client reads out of the bytes
# themselves. A .txt has no such mechanism, so the Content-Type header is the only channel, and without
# a charset parameter the client falls back to its LOCALE DEFAULT — windows-1252 across most of the
# West. Every UTF-8 em dash (`E2 80 94`) then decodes as three separate characters, `â€”`, which is
# exactly what shipped: a byte-perfect file, mislabelled.
#
# Invisible to every other check. The bytes are correct in git, correct through the export, and correct
# on the wire, so the content test on public/llms.txt passes and `curl` shows clean output (curl writes
# bytes, it does not decode them). Only something that DECODES — a browser, or the LLM crawler this file
# exists for — ever sees the damage.
#
# ⚠️ DO NOT "VERIFY" THIS RULE LOCALLY — `wrangler dev` CANNOT REPRODUCE THE BUG. The local asset
# server appends `charset=utf-8` to text/* by DEFAULT, so /llms.txt and /robots.txt both come back
# correctly labelled there with no rule at all. Production does not: it served a bare `text/plain`, and
# that gap is the whole defect. Measured 2026-07-23 — prod `content-type: text/plain` vs local
# `text/plain; charset=utf-8` on the same bytes. Production is the only authority for this rule.
#
# The `!` unsets the type Workers infers from the .txt extension before setting ours, mirroring
# /bimi/logo.svg below, so exactly one Content-Type ships however Cloudflare resolves the collision.
# Note the duplicate-Content-Type warning on that BIMI block is stronger than anything reproducible:
# dropping the `!` locally still yielded ONE header for both rules. But local is not authoritative here
# (see above), the global `nosniff` makes a malformed type unrecoverable, and /bimi/logo.svg proves the
# unset-then-set shape serves a single correct header in prod. Keep it; it costs nothing.
#
# Scoped to this file, not `/*.txt`: robots.txt is pure ASCII, which decodes identically either way.
/llms.txt
  ! Content-Type
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# The BIMI indicator. Mailbox providers (Yahoo/AOL, Fastmail, La Poste) fetch this at DELIVERY time,
# from outside any browser, so it must be unauthenticated, always-up, and correctly typed. Content-Type
# is set explicitly rather than left to extension inference: `nosniff` is on globally, so a wrong type
# is not recoverable client-side, and a malformed/mistyped indicator produces a BIMI *failure* result —
# strictly worse than publishing no BIMI record at all.
#
# Referenced by `l=` in default._bimi.{mail,billing}.webhook.co. If this path ever 404s, the logo
# silently stops rendering. Do not move it without updating those DNS records.
#
# ⚠️ THE `!` LINE IS LOAD-BEARING — same trap as /play's CSP above. `_headers` rules are ADDITIVE, and
# Workers Static Assets ALREADY infers `Content-Type: image/svg+xml` from the .svg extension. Setting it
# again without unsetting first emits the header TWICE, which a receiver sees as the invalid media type
# `image/svg+xml, image/svg+xml`. Combined with the global `nosniff`, that is unrecoverable: the
# indicator is rejected as malformed and BIMI returns a *failure* — strictly worse than no BIMI record.
# Unset, then set, so exactly one Content-Type ships.
#
# Pinned on the SVG ONLY: /bimi/* also holds apple-branded-mail.png, and a wildcard Content-Type here
# would mislabel that PNG as an SVG.
/bimi/logo.svg
  ! Content-Type
  Content-Type: image/svg+xml

/bimi/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
